.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map that is destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name with the companion type
variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred macro,
then creates the map with the default allocator and the default order for
key_t. The handle also gets the cleanup attribute, which destroys the map
on every exit from the enclosing scope, an early return included.

That attribute is the only difference from cbmap_construct(3). This macro
fits a map whose whole useful life is one block; use cbmap_construct(3)
when your code returns the handle, stores it, or destroys it somewhere the
compiler can't see.

The two-step form is cbmap_declare_scoped(3) followed by cbmap_init(3),
for a map whose configuration comes from inside the scope rather than from
the declaration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type. Every later macro reads a key through it, and the
         container takes the order of the tree from it
val_t    Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it; the library
         never passes it to the container
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and doesn't expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation
fails; when the caller has to handle a failure instead, use
cbmap_declare_scoped(3) with cbmap_init(3). The cleanup attribute means it
needs GCC or Clang.

The map keeps its elements in key order and a walk visits them in
ascending key order. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract and
cbmap_create_full(3) the order that each key type gets. For a struct key_t
the default order is a raw comparison of the key's representation, so use
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) for such a key.

The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle, so an
explicit cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope is fine too.

The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. Keys and values
are copied by assignment and C can't assign an array, so the map would
otherwise keep the address of the caller's array instead of its elements.
A struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Don't return it or store it
anywhere that lives longer than the scope: the cleanup attribute frees the
map when the scope exits, and the stored handle would then name a freed
map.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void report(int *ids, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_scoped(seen, int, int);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(seen, ids[i], one);
    }
    /* The walk visits the smallest id first */
    ccol_iter_declare(seen, it);
    for (it = ccol_begin(seen); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
        print_id(*ccol_iter_key_ptr(it));
    }
}                                 /* The macro destroys seen here */
.fi
